WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.169 --> 00:00:03.149
&gt;&gt;

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:04.169
[Applause]

00:00:04.169 --> 00:00:07.820
&gt;&gt; Well, thank you very much for that introduction, and I'm

00:00:07.839 --> 00:00:14.759
sorry I didn't bring a cello to calm you all down.

00:00:14.779 --> 00:00:19.599
I don't have a very clear understanding of what it is that

00:00:19.619 --> 00:00:23.429
you would like to hear me talk about, other than that I was

00:00:23.449 --> 00:00:27.609
told that you have an interest in development.

00:00:27.629 --> 00:00:30.419
But that interest in development might lead you to ask

00:00:30.439 --> 00:00:31.579
questions.

00:00:31.599 --> 00:00:36.229
And so, my thought was to give you a short introduction.

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:40.209
And then, leave it over to you guys to ask such questions as

00:00:40.229 --> 00:00:44.019
you think might be interesting and would allow me to

00:00:44.039 --> 00:00:47.999
contribute to your understanding -- at least in my

00:00:48.019 --> 00:00:51.859
experience and what I think is important.

00:00:51.879 --> 00:00:56.619
One was the career that I wanted to establish, which was a

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:00.089
career that I'd hoped would be in the investment banking

00:01:00.109 --> 00:01:04.810
business and which allowed me to, in fact, develop my

00:01:04.829 --> 00:01:09.159
activities in that field in countries other than my native

00:01:09.179 --> 00:01:10.650
Australia.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:16.510
And to finish out doing work in the middle of the American

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:19.599
investment banking community, and then, starting my own firm

00:01:19.619 --> 00:01:23.379
before I went off to the World Bank.

00:01:23.399 --> 00:01:30.049
And so, I was able -- because of the opportunities here --

00:01:30.069 --> 00:01:34.019
to have first the professional strand.

00:01:34.039 --> 00:01:38.739
And then, on the other side, to establish for myself a goal,

00:01:38.759 --> 00:01:42.469
which was first to spend 20 percent of my time and my

00:01:42.489 --> 00:01:43.579
income.

00:01:43.599 --> 00:01:47.799
And then, thirty percent, and ultimately more, balancing the

00:01:47.819 --> 00:01:51.789
life that I was doing between business and non-business

00:01:51.809 --> 00:01:53.469
activities.

00:01:53.489 --> 00:01:57.000
And I have to tell you, just because -- as I look at this

00:01:57.019 --> 00:02:02.159
room and think of my own background -- that if there's one

00:02:02.179 --> 00:02:06.469
thing that I would as a generality say to you, it is that,

00:02:06.489 --> 00:02:11.299
if it is possible for you, in whatever you're doing, to

00:02:11.319 --> 00:02:14.869
engage yourself in the things other than the straight

00:02:14.889 --> 00:02:18.799
business course, you will find that, not only does it enrich

00:02:18.819 --> 00:02:22.949
your life, but the truth of the matter is that it'll enrich

00:02:22.969 --> 00:02:24.349
your business.

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:28.069
Because you engage yourself in people and with people in the

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:32.909
community that have broader issues with which they're

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:34.199
engaged.

00:02:34.219 --> 00:02:37.219
And they tend to be the best people in the community -- the

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:40.599
people that have decided that it's not just a singular

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:43.819
interest in business, which is the thing that drives them.

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:48.629
But that, since business does survive within a communal

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:55.789
environment, that that aspect of duality in the life is

00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:59.469
something that I found in my life -- or was the thing that

00:02:59.489 --> 00:03:01.369
made it meaningful.

00:03:01.389 --> 00:03:04.949
But it also gave me the opportunity, as a result of

00:03:04.969 --> 00:03:10.719
activities in the field particularly, not just of the arts,

00:03:10.739 --> 00:03:14.569
but of development in which I became interested that it took

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:18.239
me into that area, for which I suppose I'm best known, which

00:03:18.259 --> 00:03:21.399
was my period at the World Bank, and subsequently in the

00:03:21.419 --> 00:03:22.419
Middle East.

00:03:22.419 --> 00:03:27.269
But today, I would like to focus on the period in the World

00:03:27.289 --> 00:03:31.679
Bank, because it has some messages that I think may be

00:03:31.699 --> 00:03:34.569
interesting to you and certainly relevant.

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:39.839
And if I were your age, I would be thinking very seriously

00:03:39.859 --> 00:03:46.779
about what it was that drove me to the Bank and what it was

00:03:46.799 --> 00:03:47.799
I learned there.

00:03:47.799 --> 00:03:51.299
The first thing I'd like to say is that, harking back to my

00:03:51.049 --> 00:03:55.679
own experience, I grew up in a world in which, for decades,

00:03:55.699 --> 00:04:03.259
there was one sixth of the world, by the year 2000 -- a

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:07.129
billion people who had 80 percent of the world's income.

00:04:07.149 --> 00:04:12.649
And then, there were 5 billion people who were in the so-

00:04:12.669 --> 00:04:16.860
called 'Developing World', that had 20 percent of the global

00:04:16.879 --> 00:04:18.670
income.

00:04:18.689 --> 00:04:24.840
And I took an interest in that 20 percent, through

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:29.009
activities and foundations and other things, largely because

00:04:29.029 --> 00:04:35.389
I couldn't understand how the world would continue in an

00:04:35.409 --> 00:04:40.759
inequitable distribution of 5 billion having 20 percent and

00:04:40.779 --> 00:04:43.699
one billion having 80 percent of the world's GDP.

00:04:43.719 --> 00:04:49.350
It stood there in the post-war years -- with varying

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:51.730
numbers, of course, in terms of the global population --

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:55.379
but, proportionately, that was the sort of relationship --

00:04:55.399 --> 00:04:59.730
this 80/20 -- which existed for decades.

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:08.740
But came the last decade of the last century and moving into

00:05:08.759 --> 00:05:13.910
this new century, we're seeing for the first time the swing,

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:19.379
which now has that 80/20 somewhere closer to 70/30 or 72/28

00:05:19.399 --> 00:05:23.350
or some such number.

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:27.649
And that is an important and rapid change which has

00:05:27.669 --> 00:05:28.669
occurred.

00:05:28.669 --> 00:05:32.980
And it's brought about a change which has been very evident

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:36.589
in the way in which international institutions function and

00:05:36.609 --> 00:05:40.870
in which, indeed, the global economy functions.

00:05:40.889 --> 00:05:45.620
So much so that as we look out, the so-called 80/20 that I

00:05:45.639 --> 00:05:50.500
and my contemporaries grew up with is now looking at a very,

00:05:50.519 --> 00:05:51.889
very different future.

00:05:51.909 --> 00:05:54.780
It is the future that you will face.

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:58.709
It is a future of 9 billion people on the planet by the year

00:05:58.729 --> 00:05:59.729
2015.

00:05:59.729 --> 00:06:04.629
Of the 3 billion extra that have been added to the planet --

00:06:04.649 --> 00:06:08.290
or will be added to the planet -- from the year 2000, about

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:12.129
a hundred million goes to the rich countries.

00:06:12.149 --> 00:06:16.360
And 2.9 billion goes to the developing countries.

00:06:16.379 --> 00:06:22.600
So come 2050, it's not one billion and five billion, it's 1.

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:28.000
1 billion and 8 billion or close to it.

00:06:28.019 --> 00:06:34.019
That is a huge change -- dramatic change.

00:06:34.039 --> 00:06:39.139
The other consequential change is that the structure of the

00:06:39.159 --> 00:06:47.720
economy is being driven both by the population development

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:50.959
and also by modern technology, which has allowed the

00:06:50.979 --> 00:06:55.899
conveyance of ideas and innovation to move to the developing

00:06:55.919 --> 00:06:58.009
world.

00:06:58.029 --> 00:07:02.620
And so, the projections today are that that old 80/20, with

00:07:02.639 --> 00:07:06.279
which I grew up for decades, will become 35/65.

00:07:06.299 --> 00:07:12.439
Thirty-five percent for the billion one in the rich

00:07:12.459 --> 00:07:17.620
countries and 65 percent for the people in the developing

00:07:17.639 --> 00:07:19.219
countries.

00:07:19.239 --> 00:07:23.829
That is turning the world on its head, in terms of the world

00:07:23.849 --> 00:07:25.099
that I grew up in.

00:07:25.099 --> 00:07:30.120
But it is the world that you're going to work in.

00:07:30.139 --> 00:07:32.920
And it is not a trivial change.

00:07:32.939 --> 00:07:38.170
It is a change of monumental importance.

00:07:38.189 --> 00:07:44.459
So much so that, by 2050, China and India -- the two

00:07:44.479 --> 00:07:48.839
countries that I have -- of course, the leading countries in

00:07:48.859 --> 00:07:53.050
the developing world, will constitute 50 percent of the

00:07:53.069 --> 00:07:57.920
global GDP. 50 percent of the global GDP.

00:07:57.939 --> 00:08:00.959
That is the consensus estimate.

00:08:00.979 --> 00:08:04.759
But let's say we're off 5 percent, it's 45 percent of the

00:08:04.779 --> 00:08:06.529
global GDP.

00:08:06.549 --> 00:08:10.490
It's nonetheless a monumental switch in terms of economic

00:08:10.509 --> 00:08:12.409
power.

00:08:12.429 --> 00:08:16.509
It happened last in 1815 when China and India were 15

00:08:16.529 --> 00:08:18.680
percent of the global GDP.

00:08:18.699 --> 00:08:21.959
And it happened before that in the year 1500.

00:08:21.979 --> 00:08:25.409
And in the period from 1815, at various times, it's been

00:08:25.429 --> 00:08:28.899
approaching that, but with communism and with the change in

00:08:28.919 --> 00:08:31.509
governance in China and with the greater development in

00:08:31.529 --> 00:08:35.309
terms of Industrial Revolution in the West, you got to a

00:08:35.329 --> 00:08:39.039
point where, after World War II, China and India were,

00:08:39.059 --> 00:08:42.110
together, 2 percent of the global GDP.

00:08:42.129 --> 00:08:46.110
So the growth -- in terms of China and India and of Asia

00:08:46.129 --> 00:08:52.990
generally -- is such that, it is not just some modulation of

00:08:53.009 --> 00:08:59.070
former trends, it is an absolutely fundamental change in the

00:08:59.090 --> 00:09:01.960
way the world is balanced.

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:10.029
I remember I was at a meeting of the G-7, G-8, the first

00:09:10.049 --> 00:09:15.740
time that the people from the developing world -- leaders --

00:09:15.759 --> 00:09:17.370
were invited.

00:09:17.389 --> 00:09:21.289
Seven or eight leaders were invited by Jacque Chirac to a

00:09:21.309 --> 00:09:23.779
meeting that was held in France.

00:09:23.799 --> 00:09:27.549
You'd be familiar with the fact that the G-7, G-8, meet once

00:09:27.079 --> 00:09:35.069
a year in June or July, and generally -- until recently --

00:09:35.089 --> 00:09:37.839
decide what they think's going to happen with the world.

00:09:37.419 --> 00:09:40.580
And then, they might tell a few people outside.

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:43.779
Well, it changed at that meeting.

00:09:43.799 --> 00:09:46.929
And I remember that the first person to speak was Hu Jintao

00:09:46.949 --> 00:09:51.860
from China, who made a very elegant speech about China and

00:09:51.879 --> 00:09:55.110
about the growth and about interdependence between China and

00:09:55.129 --> 00:09:57.100
the rest of the world.

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:01.289
The prime minister, Bajbai, from India then spoke -- I

00:10:01.309 --> 00:10:03.960
think, probably eloquently, but I couldn't understand his

00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:08.960
English very well, so I can only assume that it was another

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:09.980
brilliant speech.

00:10:09.980 --> 00:10:17.110
But then, President Lula from Brazil got up and said rather

00:10:17.129 --> 00:10:20.500
charmingly, "If only my parents could see me now.

00:10:20.519 --> 00:10:25.179
I came from a very poor family and to see myself here with

00:10:25.199 --> 00:10:29.330
you, President Chirac, and with you, the prime minister of

00:10:29.350 --> 00:10:31.860
England, and you, the head of Germany, and you -- he

00:10:31.879 --> 00:10:34.069
detailed the people that were in the room.

00:10:34.089 --> 00:10:37.089
He didn't mention the president of the World Bank, I want

00:10:36.589 --> 00:10:37.589
you to know.

00:10:37.589 --> 00:10:38.589
[laughter]

00:10:37.589 --> 00:10:42.309
But he did mention the other minor people that were there.

00:10:42.329 --> 00:10:46.939
And then, he said, "But I'd like you to consider something,

00:10:46.959 --> 00:10:52.009
and maybe next year, you should have your meeting in Brazil.

00:10:52.029 --> 00:10:54.779
And the reason you should do that is because you should

00:10:54.519 --> 00:10:57.590
start getting used to the fact that, in another ten or

00:10:57.610 --> 00:11:01.879
twelve years, five of you are not going to be here.

00:11:01.899 --> 00:11:02.899
[laughter]

00:11:02.899 --> 00:11:08.279
And you will be replaced by my friend from China and my

00:11:08.299 --> 00:11:10.419
friend from India and by myself.

00:11:10.439 --> 00:11:15.000
And we'd like you to get used to it, so that you can come

00:11:15.019 --> 00:11:16.019
see us."

00:11:16.019 --> 00:11:20.830
Well, it was sort of prescient, because as you know, when

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:25.590
the economic crisis hit, very quickly, it moved from a G-8

00:11:25.610 --> 00:11:27.929
issue to a G-20 issue.

00:11:27.949 --> 00:11:32.230
And now, the G-8 is part of the past and the G-20 is the new

00:11:32.250 --> 00:11:34.000
institution that is there.

00:11:34.019 --> 00:11:41.379
And it again fits into the prospective development of which

00:11:41.399 --> 00:11:45.549
I spoke, which is that the weighting of the planet is now

00:11:45.569 --> 00:11:49.789
moving in different directions.

00:11:49.809 --> 00:11:54.279
Until recently, 'til we had this crisis, the United States

00:11:54.299 --> 00:11:57.970
had a sort of 10-trillion consumption.

00:11:57.990 --> 00:12:00.470
European union had nine.

00:12:00.490 --> 00:12:03.980
And Asia and the rest of the developing world had a little

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:05.000
less than $5 trillion.

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:08.659
So it was ten, nine, five.

00:12:08.679 --> 00:12:15.830
And the middle class in the world -- of whom there are today

00:12:15.850 --> 00:12:19.980
or couple years ago -- was a billion and a half, roughly, in

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:21.309
the middle class.

00:12:21.329 --> 00:12:25.909
A billion was in the rich countries in that group that I

00:12:25.929 --> 00:12:27.439
spoke of.

00:12:27.459 --> 00:12:32.379
And about half a billion in the middle class characterizes

00:12:32.399 --> 00:12:36.409
incomes of between ten dollars and a hundred dollars per

00:12:36.429 --> 00:12:48.590
capita per week -- that this amount of money was

00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:54.779
demonstrated to be heavily leveraged to those richer

00:12:54.799 --> 00:12:56.569
countries.

00:12:56.589 --> 00:13:00.069
By 2030, the estimate now is that there will be 3 billion

00:13:00.089 --> 00:13:04.710
people in the middle class, and of that 3 billion, two-

00:13:04.730 --> 00:13:08.939
thirds of it will be in Asia. Two-thirds of it.

00:13:08.959 --> 00:13:14.980
So this is a switch from being a fraction, being less than a

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:17.730
third, to being two-thirds.

00:13:17.750 --> 00:13:20.049
And that is a pretty dramatic shift.

00:13:20.069 --> 00:13:23.179
You'll have a middle class in China of a billion people.

00:13:23.199 --> 00:13:27.949
Billion people by 2050 -- middle class.

00:13:27.969 --> 00:13:34.370
So these are not trivial changes in the outlook.

00:13:34.389 --> 00:13:37.779
These are tectonic shifts in terms of the way the planet

00:13:37.799 --> 00:13:39.710
works.

00:13:39.730 --> 00:13:43.480
And as I said to you -- as I look at all of you -- in my

00:13:42.799 --> 00:13:46.669
generation, we didn't have to think about that.

00:13:46.689 --> 00:13:48.889
In the dean's generation.

00:13:48.909 --> 00:13:54.210
It was 80/20, or 78/22 or something like that.

00:13:54.230 --> 00:14:00.329
It was a balance where you knew you were the rich countries

00:14:00.349 --> 00:14:03.819
-- the powerful countries -- and all the organs of running

00:14:03.839 --> 00:14:07.379
the world were designed to accommodate that fact.

00:14:07.399 --> 00:14:11.129
But the world that you're going into is a very, very

00:14:11.149 --> 00:14:12.949
different one.

00:14:12.969 --> 00:14:17.529
And it's not something that is going to be turned back.

00:14:17.549 --> 00:14:21.129
It's something that will happen. It may not happen lineally.

00:14:21.149 --> 00:14:25.189
There may be events that will occur that will disrupt it for

00:14:25.209 --> 00:14:29.289
three years or five years that may not be exactly 65/35.

00:14:29.309 --> 00:14:36.049
It may be a variation of that, but directionally, it is very

00:14:36.069 --> 00:14:37.069
clear.

00:14:37.069 --> 00:14:41.699
And there are a couple of observations that I think are

00:14:41.719 --> 00:14:42.860
interesting beyond that.

00:14:42.879 --> 00:14:48.799
But before I get to the observations, let me make one other

00:14:48.819 --> 00:14:51.340
point about the planet.

00:14:51.360 --> 00:14:56.309
And that is that there -- and I say this in the presence of

00:14:56.329 --> 00:15:01.000
the dean -- because in Africa, of the 8 or 9 hundred million

00:15:01.019 --> 00:15:04.750
people that are now there, that moved over to 2 billion

00:15:04.769 --> 00:15:06.789
people by 2050.

00:15:06.809 --> 00:15:09.949
Two billion out of nine billion.

00:15:09.969 --> 00:15:16.230
And the estimates are that the average per-capita income for

00:15:16.250 --> 00:15:19.500
people in Africa at that stage will be between two and three

00:15:19.519 --> 00:15:23.370
thousand dollars per capita.

00:15:23.389 --> 00:15:28.019
The same projections would have China and India between

00:15:28.039 --> 00:15:32.019
thirty and forty thousand dollars per capita.

00:15:32.039 --> 00:15:36.370
So you have two to three thousand, thirty to forty thousand.

00:15:36.389 --> 00:15:39.559
And countries in the so-called 'rich' world -- United

00:15:39.579 --> 00:15:44.309
States, European countries -- somewhere between ninety and a

00:15:44.329 --> 00:15:47.449
hundred thousand dollars per capita.

00:15:47.469 --> 00:15:50.219
I'm not giving you these figures with any sense of

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:51.000
mathematical accuracy.

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:54.720
They may be off a bit, but directionally, they're right.

00:15:54.740 --> 00:15:59.860
So if you have 2 billion people living in a continent with

00:15:59.879 --> 00:16:03.259
two to three thousand dollars per capita, have China and

00:16:03.279 --> 00:16:08.559
India with more people -- three to four billion -- three

00:16:08.579 --> 00:16:12.699
billion -- between thirty and forty thousand dollars per

00:16:12.719 --> 00:16:14.470
capita.

00:16:14.490 --> 00:16:19.439
And you have the rest of the world somewhere either north of

00:16:19.459 --> 00:16:23.799
that or in the middle class somewhere between the two

00:16:23.819 --> 00:16:28.959
countries, the two areas of the world.

00:16:28.979 --> 00:16:33.389
You have a basis for instability.

00:16:33.409 --> 00:16:39.740
Africa is not a continent which is any longer isolated.

00:16:39.759 --> 00:16:43.649
It's not a place where people are uninformed.

00:16:43.669 --> 00:16:49.309
It's the fastest-growing market for cellular phones.

00:16:49.329 --> 00:16:52.269
Information, as you would well know -- whether it's in the

00:16:52.289 --> 00:16:56.370
townships or wherever it is -- now passes very quickly.

00:16:56.389 --> 00:16:58.389
And that's not just in South Africa.

00:16:57.990 --> 00:17:01.980
That's in the 53 countries in sub-Saharan Africa.

00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:06.009
And this is not an issue which is going to go away.

00:17:06.029 --> 00:17:09.829
Nor is it an issue which is trivial to those of us that live

00:17:09.849 --> 00:17:12.149
as we do here.

00:17:12.169 --> 00:17:17.359
This is a major issue, which is not being confronted, and

00:17:17.379 --> 00:17:20.629
which you will have to confront at some stage.

00:17:20.649 --> 00:17:26.470
And that gets into a second issue which I would like to

00:17:26.490 --> 00:17:31.480
touch on, which is, What is happening with the rest of the

00:17:31.500 --> 00:17:37.430
world as it looks at this changing dynamic?

00:17:37.450 --> 00:17:40.660
Having worked in the World Bank and had some familiarity

00:17:40.680 --> 00:17:45.359
with the whole issue of aid, I know just how tough it is to

00:17:45.379 --> 00:17:50.609
get people in the rich world to really understand what the

00:17:50.629 --> 00:17:54.649
responsibilities are, not just as a matter of conscience,

00:17:54.669 --> 00:17:58.440
but as a matter of enlightened self-interest to try and help

00:17:58.460 --> 00:18:02.059
development in the developing world.

00:18:02.079 --> 00:18:09.129
There was established decades ago a 0.7 target for foreign

00:18:09.149 --> 00:18:10.329
aid.

00:18:10.349 --> 00:18:15.970
My former colleague from this activity -- who is here from a

00:18:15.990 --> 00:18:20.269
different part of Stanford, as I understand -- knows this.

00:18:20.289 --> 00:18:25.680
And we're now, really, after a lot of speeches and a lot of

00:18:25.700 --> 00:18:29.629
activity -- we're starting to get some momentum before the

00:18:29.649 --> 00:18:33.970
economic crisis of 15 months ago, which we're now maybe

00:18:33.990 --> 00:18:36.299
getting out of, to a degree.

00:18:36.319 --> 00:18:41.000
But it's now not the 0.7.

00:18:41.019 --> 00:18:45.680
It's about 0.2 that we've reached.

00:18:45.700 --> 00:18:48.700
And there are a lot of statements that are still being made,

00:18:48.149 --> 00:18:53.970
but in terms of the actual money which is flowing, if you x

00:18:53.990 --> 00:18:59.049
out the amount of money that are going to the travel spots

00:18:59.069 --> 00:19:06.190
in the world today, there is very, very little development.

00:19:06.210 --> 00:19:09.250
And in fact, the per-capita contributions to Africa have

00:19:09.269 --> 00:19:13.779
been declining steadily for the last 10 or 15 years.

00:19:13.799 --> 00:19:18.519
So, one of the tragedies is that there is great analysis of

00:19:18.539 --> 00:19:19.539
the issues.

00:19:19.539 --> 00:19:23.569
There's great understanding of the issues.

00:19:23.589 --> 00:19:28.109
But these, like the environment, are long-term issues.

00:19:28.129 --> 00:19:32.500
And political decision-making is essentially short-term.

00:19:32.519 --> 00:19:36.099
And so, you have political decisions which are being taken -

00:19:36.119 --> 00:19:42.579
- in terms of this demonstrable development in terms of

00:19:42.599 --> 00:19:46.799
global balance -- that certainly, so far as western

00:19:46.819 --> 00:19:50.099
countries are concerned, is not being met.

00:19:50.119 --> 00:19:54.819
What is interesting is to see how the dynamic between Africa

00:19:54.839 --> 00:19:58.529
and China and Africa and India is developing.

00:19:58.549 --> 00:20:02.829
Three years ago, for the first time, the summit meetings of

00:20:02.849 --> 00:20:07.440
the African leaders was held in Beijing.

00:20:07.460 --> 00:20:12.299
And they swore after that that it was unlikely that they

00:20:12.319 --> 00:20:16.159
would ever meet again in a western country.

00:20:16.179 --> 00:20:20.690
Simultaneously, 400 African businessmen met in New Delhi.

00:20:20.710 --> 00:20:25.629
And each led to an understanding, on the part of both the

00:20:25.649 --> 00:20:29.440
Chinese and the Indians, of having a dimension of their

00:20:29.460 --> 00:20:31.599
activities geared towards Africa.

00:20:31.619 --> 00:20:34.460
Of course, there's been an Indian community in Africa --

00:20:34.480 --> 00:20:36.480
particularly, in East Africa -- for a long time.

00:20:36.169 --> 00:20:40.519
And there are now 750,000 Chinese in Africa.

00:20:40.539 --> 00:20:44.289
And if you're in the business that I'm in, I was recently in

00:20:43.789 --> 00:20:49.500
an African country where we're involved in a real estate

00:20:49.519 --> 00:20:54.500
development, we went up-country to visit this real estate

00:20:54.519 --> 00:20:59.029
development and -- which was near a university.

00:20:59.049 --> 00:21:02.099
And I was astonished to find that everybody on the team that

00:21:02.119 --> 00:21:07.379
was doing the development was Chinese -- the architects, the

00:21:07.399 --> 00:21:11.029
town planners, the builders -- everyone was Chinese.

00:21:11.049 --> 00:21:16.269
So if you go to Africa today, you don't see people who come

00:21:16.289 --> 00:21:18.769
from western business schools.

00:21:18.789 --> 00:21:22.339
You see people from China and from India.

00:21:22.359 --> 00:21:24.519
And I just raise that as a point.

00:21:24.539 --> 00:21:28.289
And the second point I would make is that, in terms of China

00:21:28.309 --> 00:21:34.399
and India -- in terms of their development -- I was trying

00:21:34.419 --> 00:21:37.430
to get the most recent numbers, and they may be in my

00:21:37.450 --> 00:21:40.569
Blackberry, but I haven't looked.

00:21:40.589 --> 00:21:46.909
But the numbers in 2007 were the following: There were 110,

00:21:46.929 --> 00:21:51.940
000 Chinese studying in the United States.

00:21:51.960 --> 00:21:56.909
There are now over a hundred thousand Indians studying in

00:21:56.929 --> 00:21:57.929
the United States.

00:21:57.929 --> 00:22:02.500
So I look around the room and suppose it's not surprising.

00:22:02.519 --> 00:22:08.819
There were 11,200 Americans studying in China, and 2,800

00:22:08.839 --> 00:22:11.740
studying in India.

00:22:11.759 --> 00:22:18.480
This is madness. It's just madness.

00:22:18.500 --> 00:22:27.519
And it's a tragedy, in terms of the potential for our young

00:22:27.539 --> 00:22:31.519
people that are still being guided to look towards Europe,

00:22:31.539 --> 00:22:35.149
to look towards graduate work in British or European

00:22:35.169 --> 00:22:44.309
universities, when the world is telling them that the

00:22:44.329 --> 00:22:45.329
dimensions have changed notably.

00:22:45.329 --> 00:22:48.329
I was very interested to hear that there's been a recent

00:22:48.079 --> 00:22:51.240
trip made by some of your students to Asia and another one

00:22:51.259 --> 00:22:53.450
to the Middle East.

00:22:53.470 --> 00:22:58.889
But, what is needed in today's world is for us to come up

00:22:58.909 --> 00:23:04.220
with what is the last thing that I'll touch on, which is

00:23:04.240 --> 00:23:08.599
this: Historically, the western countries were able to stay

00:23:08.619 --> 00:23:12.569
ahead, firstly, because of manufacturing.

00:23:12.589 --> 00:23:19.690
Well, that got taken out, and manufacturing moved to Asia.

00:23:19.710 --> 00:23:23.799
The second thing that happened after that was that, in

00:23:23.819 --> 00:23:30.349
service industries, it moved to the western countries.

00:23:30.369 --> 00:23:35.329
And now, that's been taken out in terms of Asian, really,

00:23:35.349 --> 00:23:38.250
dominance in the service areas.

00:23:38.269 --> 00:23:43.279
And thirdly was, in technology, we were able to stay ahead,

00:23:43.299 --> 00:23:46.299
but as is evident to you -- and I'm sure from your

00:23:46.089 --> 00:23:49.169
colleagues and people you know -- the technological advance

00:23:49.189 --> 00:23:52.559
has now shifted as well.

00:23:52.579 --> 00:23:56.329
So, the challenge for our country is, What the hell is it

00:23:56.349 --> 00:23:59.849
that's going to be left for us if Asia's eating our lunch

00:23:59.689 --> 00:24:03.189
and dinner in terms of the things that we used to be able to

00:24:02.250 --> 00:24:03.250
do?

00:24:03.250 --> 00:24:05.789
And it's not just the United States.

00:24:05.809 --> 00:24:10.579
It is truly that group of the so-called 'billion plus' that

00:24:10.599 --> 00:24:13.690
were previously the dominant factor who had 80 percent of

00:24:13.710 --> 00:24:16.230
the world GDP.

00:24:16.250 --> 00:24:20.559
And so, I leave you with that issue, because as people that

00:24:20.579 --> 00:24:24.029
are going out into the world, if it were me today, the

00:24:24.049 --> 00:24:27.399
number one thing that I would be thinking about, which is

00:24:27.419 --> 00:24:31.909
different from when I grew up, is that the 80/20 rule, which

00:24:31.929 --> 00:24:36.649
I had comfortably in my hip pocket, is going to be a 35/65

00:24:36.669 --> 00:24:39.519
rule.

00:24:39.539 --> 00:24:45.369
And that puts a challenge of dramatic proportions to anybody

00:24:45.389 --> 00:24:49.339
who's at a business school today or graduating.

00:24:49.359 --> 00:24:53.000
And so, I was told that I should go for about this time, and

00:24:53.019 --> 00:24:55.769
I would then be peppered with questions that would be so

00:24:55.779 --> 00:24:58.279
intelligent that I wouldn't be able to answer them.

00:24:58.029 --> 00:24:59.029
[laughter]

00:24:58.029 --> 00:25:03.309
So, if there are questions, I'd be delighted to have a shot

00:25:03.329 --> 00:25:06.980
at them.

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:08.000
Yes, ma'am.

00:25:07.460 --> 00:25:10.210
Q My question is, How would you respond to people like

00:25:10.210 --> 00:25:15.539
Dambisa Moyo's arguments that aid to Africa is actually a

00:25:15.559 --> 00:25:17.700
bad thing and should be stopped?

00:25:17.720 --> 00:25:23.500
A I think that the methodology of aid is the thing that

00:25:23.519 --> 00:25:24.710
should be looked at.

00:25:24.730 --> 00:25:29.619
I think that Africa, first of all, is not a homogenous

00:25:29.639 --> 00:25:31.779
place.

00:25:31.799 --> 00:25:37.669
With 53 countries -- and there are some that I think are

00:25:37.689 --> 00:25:41.220
taking to themselves responsibility for management and

00:25:41.240 --> 00:25:42.240
governance.

00:25:42.240 --> 00:25:45.240
And there are some that are just, very sadly, a long way

00:25:45.240 --> 00:25:50.369
away from it -- essentially corrupt, impossible places to

00:25:50.389 --> 00:25:52.179
have management.

00:25:52.199 --> 00:25:55.839
And so, I think, what you need to do is to be selective.

00:25:55.859 --> 00:26:00.079
The first thing that I have become very impressed by is that

00:26:00.099 --> 00:26:04.519
there are now a growing number of young Africans.

00:26:04.539 --> 00:26:07.789
Although, I look around the room; I don't see too many here.

00:26:07.619 --> 00:26:11.069
A growing number of young Africans who are going overseas

00:26:11.089 --> 00:26:13.490
for education.

00:26:13.509 --> 00:26:15.069
And these are black Africans.

00:26:15.089 --> 00:26:18.579
These are not just white, South Africans or white other

00:26:18.599 --> 00:26:19.879
Africans.

00:26:19.899 --> 00:26:21.149
And they're coming back.

00:26:20.939 --> 00:26:25.039
And they are trying to find room for themselves, both in

00:26:25.059 --> 00:26:27.500
business and in government.

00:26:27.519 --> 00:26:32.970
And even in my decade at the Bank, I saw a significant move

00:26:32.990 --> 00:26:39.240
in the development of this core of young Africans.

00:26:39.259 --> 00:26:42.389
And then, the question is, To what extent can they be backed

00:26:42.409 --> 00:26:43.980
by the governments?

00:26:44.000 --> 00:26:47.980
And to what extent is there a framework in which there is a

00:26:48.000 --> 00:26:50.869
judicial system, in which there is governance, in which

00:26:50.889 --> 00:26:58.250
there is an attempt to counteract corruption and to create

00:26:58.269 --> 00:27:00.940
some sort of meritocracy?

00:27:00.960 --> 00:27:03.529
And that is happening in some countries in Africa.

00:27:03.549 --> 00:27:07.789
I would say you could see movement in 15 or 20 of the

00:27:07.809 --> 00:27:09.549
countries of the 52, 53.

00:27:09.569 --> 00:27:16.460
And I think it is a transAfrican move now.

00:27:16.480 --> 00:27:21.349
The thing that I failed at doing was trying to get the

00:27:21.369 --> 00:27:27.509
African leaders, who were the most enlightened, to try and -

00:27:27.529 --> 00:27:32.809
- we tried to split the continent into quartiles and have

00:27:32.829 --> 00:27:36.279
the best of leadership deal with the countries in each of

00:27:36.299 --> 00:27:40.619
these sectors led by Africans -- enlightened African

00:27:40.639 --> 00:27:43.039
leaders.

00:27:43.059 --> 00:27:48.089
But unfortunately, we failed, because the presidential

00:27:48.109 --> 00:27:51.009
system in these countries and the leadership in all the

00:27:51.029 --> 00:27:56.240
countries just made it impossible for us at that time to try

00:27:56.259 --> 00:28:01.119
and encourage the development of the best practices to come

00:28:01.139 --> 00:28:03.490
to the top.

00:28:03.509 --> 00:28:06.929
But I'm not totally pessimistic, because there is a change.

00:28:06.949 --> 00:28:12.109
But it's not nearly as quick as I would like to see it.

00:28:12.129 --> 00:28:15.289
And I think that aid going to countries -- to answer

00:28:15.309 --> 00:28:18.799
specifically your question -- aid going to countries that

00:28:18.819 --> 00:28:24.169
will be corrupt and that will waste it is, I hope, going to

00:28:24.189 --> 00:28:26.539
become a thing of the past.

00:28:26.559 --> 00:28:29.490
You'll find humanitarian relief going into those countries,

00:28:29.509 --> 00:28:32.980
but it will not be run by the government.

00:28:33.000 --> 00:28:37.439
And there needs to be a system of 'carrots' and 'sticks' to

00:28:37.459 --> 00:28:41.740
try to get the countries to change their practice so that

00:28:41.759 --> 00:28:44.579
the best countries can be rewarded.

00:28:44.599 --> 00:28:47.599
That, by the way, was something that was brought in when the

00:28:47.339 --> 00:28:49.619
Bush Administration, the best countries to be rewarded.

00:28:49.639 --> 00:28:53.519
And say to the others, "It's just not acceptable to steal

00:28:53.539 --> 00:28:57.200
the money that we're giving."

00:28:57.220 --> 00:29:00.720
It's going to be a tough road, but I believe we've started

00:29:00.059 --> 00:29:01.059
on it.

00:29:01.059 --> 00:29:04.839
And I believe that the best advocates of the enlightened

00:29:04.859 --> 00:29:08.909
African leaders themselves -- not westerners -- I believe

00:29:08.929 --> 00:29:19.509
that that is now happening. Yes, sir.

00:29:19.529 --> 00:29:22.819
Q Considering the huge amount of change, especially away

00:29:22.839 --> 00:29:26.089
from the U.S, that you talked about, can you stress how you

00:29:26.089 --> 00:29:27.839
think the World Bank needs to change?

00:29:27.839 --> 00:29:30.589
And then, also concerning the U.S. president's ability to

00:29:30.339 --> 00:29:33.089
appoint the leader of the World Bank, does that hinder the

00:29:33.089 --> 00:29:34.089
related change away from

00:29:34.089 --> 00:29:35.089
[inaudible]

00:29:34.089 --> 00:29:37.089
A I think that the old practices -- I'm sure everyone knows

00:29:36.839 --> 00:29:39.339
here -- was that the Europeans appointed the head of the

00:29:39.339 --> 00:29:41.480
International Monetary Fund, and characteristically, the

00:29:41.500 --> 00:29:45.730
United States appointed the president of the World Bank.

00:29:45.750 --> 00:29:48.769
I think that is now a thing of the past.

00:29:48.789 --> 00:29:55.099
I think that has changed, and that, it will be an open

00:29:55.119 --> 00:29:56.419
competition next time.

00:29:56.439 --> 00:30:03.619
Part of the reason for this is that the appointment of the

00:30:03.639 --> 00:30:06.889
person of the president of the World Bank was at a time when

00:30:06.889 --> 00:30:09.279
the U.S. economy was dominant.

00:30:09.299 --> 00:30:11.849
Today, that's no longer the case.

00:30:11.869 --> 00:30:14.869
As I said to you, it used to be ten, nine, five.

00:30:14.379 --> 00:30:19.109
Or ten trillion, nine trillion, five trillion for the rest

00:30:19.129 --> 00:30:20.129
of the world.

00:30:20.129 --> 00:30:21.899
That is changing.

00:30:21.919 --> 00:30:27.519
And, as it changes, I have personally witnessed, in my own

00:30:27.539 --> 00:30:33.549
time at the Bank, a definitive change in the way in which

00:30:33.569 --> 00:30:35.569
groups of countries come together to challenge American

00:30:35.379 --> 00:30:36.379
dominance.

00:30:36.379 --> 00:30:40.919
First, it was the Europeans. And then, it's the Brit

00:30:40.939 --> 00:30:42.480
countries.

00:30:42.500 --> 00:30:45.099
But it's very, very clear.

00:30:45.119 --> 00:30:49.220
And so, my own judgment is that the next president of the

00:30:49.240 --> 00:30:52.689
World Bank is -- could be American, if there's someone

00:30:52.709 --> 00:30:53.709
dramatic.

00:30:53.709 --> 00:30:55.980
But it will not be as a right.

00:30:56.000 --> 00:31:00.639
It will be because that person is notably the best person

00:31:00.659 --> 00:31:03.980
that you could have.

00:31:04.000 --> 00:31:06.960
But I think that, in both institutions, they're now

00:31:06.980 --> 00:31:08.809
internationalized.

00:31:08.829 --> 00:31:12.799
And it is much more likely that you'll have the 'best in

00:31:12.819 --> 00:31:26.679
class' for both the Bank and the Fund. Yes.

00:31:26.699 --> 00:31:29.699
Q Can you talk about the involvement of China and India in

00:31:29.699 --> 00:31:30.699
the development of Africa.

00:31:30.699 --> 00:31:32.449
And their business connections and political connections.

00:31:32.449 --> 00:31:35.449
And the World Bank is openly criticized for not tying aid to

00:31:35.449 --> 00:31:37.949
environmental goals, to not tying aid to human rights goals.

00:31:37.949 --> 00:31:40.699
And the World Bank has been trying to move in that

00:31:39.949 --> 00:31:40.949
direction.

00:31:40.949 --> 00:31:43.949
At the same time, now you have a second layer or second

00:31:43.949 --> 00:31:46.949
source of aid or some form of capital that doesn't really

00:31:46.699 --> 00:31:48.199
impose that conditionality as of yet.

00:31:48.199 --> 00:31:51.199
And I was just wondering what do you think of that aspect

00:31:51.199 --> 00:31:53.699
and what does that mean for the next twenty years?

00:31:53.699 --> 00:31:56.699
A Well, I think the first thing is to challenge the first

00:31:56.699 --> 00:31:59.199
assumption, which is inherent in what you're saying, which

00:31:58.949 --> 00:32:01.599
is that aid was tied into environmental or human rights'

00:32:01.619 --> 00:32:03.839
conditionality.

00:32:03.859 --> 00:32:07.649
And I think, personally, that that was more nominal than it

00:32:07.669 --> 00:32:09.000
was real.

00:32:09.019 --> 00:32:13.269
It was increasingly expressed in the last ten years.

00:32:13.289 --> 00:32:16.809
But the truth of the matter is that I don't think that there

00:32:16.829 --> 00:32:20.779
was the hammer to come in to insist.

00:32:20.799 --> 00:32:26.829
There were all sorts of methodologies that we used to

00:32:26.849 --> 00:32:30.849
produce arguments as to why you were environmentally moving

00:32:30.869 --> 00:32:35.129
forward or you were being more innovative in terms of

00:32:35.149 --> 00:32:38.909
opening up the society to people.

00:32:38.929 --> 00:32:43.309
I think that it's not something that will change instantly.

00:32:43.329 --> 00:32:46.750
In history, nothing, by the way, has changed instantly --

00:32:46.769 --> 00:32:49.970
not in terms of Europe and not in terms of anywhere.

00:32:49.990 --> 00:32:54.730
But I think that what we are on is, on a clear path, which I

00:32:54.750 --> 00:32:59.299
am sad to say has been arrested -- at least in terms of the

00:32:59.319 --> 00:33:03.109
environment -- by the recent, economic downturn.

00:33:03.129 --> 00:33:10.099
Copenhagen was a colossal disappointment in my judgment,

00:33:10.119 --> 00:33:14.529
certainly relative to what many of us had hoped for.

00:33:14.549 --> 00:33:28.019
And the truth of the matter is that, leaders in countries,

00:33:28.039 --> 00:33:30.889
the rich countries -- in terms of the allocation of their

00:33:30.909 --> 00:33:39.750
resources -- focus appropriately on what are their domestic

00:33:39.769 --> 00:33:41.960
needs.

00:33:41.980 --> 00:33:45.399
And because of a downturn in the global economy for the

00:33:45.419 --> 00:33:50.490
first time in 60 years last year, it has exacerbated the

00:33:50.509 --> 00:33:53.759
issue about, What the hell do we do in terms of getting our

00:33:53.619 --> 00:33:56.029
own economies going?

00:33:56.049 --> 00:33:59.549
When that happens, people in Africa -- or people in less-

00:33:59.569 --> 00:34:03.819
developed countries -- become secondary.

00:34:03.839 --> 00:34:08.489
And so, what you tend to get are luminous statements, but

00:34:08.509 --> 00:34:11.000
not a lot of action.

00:34:11.019 --> 00:34:15.099
And that's why I said to you before, in terms of Africa, the

00:34:15.119 --> 00:34:19.469
contributions over the last 20 years, per capita, have been

00:34:19.489 --> 00:34:21.059
declining.

00:34:21.079 --> 00:34:25.940
Not trivially, but significantly -- 50 percent decline.

00:34:25.960 --> 00:34:30.690
And so, I think that we need two things: We need a

00:34:30.710 --> 00:34:33.039
turnaround in the economy.

00:34:33.059 --> 00:34:38.400
And then, hopefully, we need some global leadership that is

00:34:38.420 --> 00:34:43.759
thinking beyond a four-year term of political office.

00:34:43.779 --> 00:34:47.159
I haven't seen that in my lifetime yet.

00:34:47.179 --> 00:34:51.150
One of the great advocates, by the way, in my years at the

00:34:51.170 --> 00:34:55.549
Bank was Gordon Brown as chancellor and even as prime

00:34:55.569 --> 00:34:57.130
minister.

00:34:57.150 --> 00:35:01.079
But Gordon, now, has a problem with the U.K.

00:35:01.099 --> 00:35:04.099
And so, if he comes out and starts talking about how much

00:35:04.029 --> 00:35:08.639
money he's going to give to the developing world, it's not

00:35:08.659 --> 00:35:11.409
going to help him in the next election -- if anything would

00:35:10.869 --> 00:35:13.400
help him in the next election.

00:35:13.420 --> 00:35:18.409
I hope something will, but -- so I'm afraid that we're

00:35:18.429 --> 00:35:23.759
caught up in this terrible path in the 'short-termism'

00:35:23.779 --> 00:35:28.289
against something which is an inevitable long-term problem.

00:35:28.309 --> 00:35:31.949
And nowhere is that long-term problem more evident or likely

00:35:31.969 --> 00:35:36.279
to hit us than in the environmental issue.

00:35:36.299 --> 00:35:39.429
Environment is something that, you know, we're not going to

00:35:39.449 --> 00:35:42.449
be able to decide in four years we're going to fix.

00:35:42.199 --> 00:35:45.449
It'll be more difficult to fix if we leave it 12 years --

00:35:45.199 --> 00:35:47.029
you can't fix it.

00:35:47.049 --> 00:35:52.929
This is not -- this is a problem all you guys are going to

00:35:52.949 --> 00:35:53.949
have to deal with.

00:35:53.949 --> 00:35:54.949
[laughter]

00:35:53.949 --> 00:35:57.199
I'll be six feet under or something by that stage. Yes, ma'

00:35:56.389 --> 00:35:57.389
am.

00:35:57.389 --> 00:36:00.389
Q I'm just curious what you see is the most promising

00:36:00.139 --> 00:36:02.139
practices in terms of poverty reduction in Africa.

00:36:02.139 --> 00:36:05.139
And if you had a magic wand that you could focus attention

00:36:05.139 --> 00:36:07.889
and investment on a couple key levers, what would they be?

00:36:07.889 --> 00:36:14.449
A I would be very supportive of nongovernmental

00:36:14.469 --> 00:36:16.250
intervention.

00:36:16.269 --> 00:36:20.969
I think that the role of civil society is crucial.

00:36:20.989 --> 00:36:26.110
And I think that the role, in terms of funding --

00:36:26.130 --> 00:36:31.349
particularly for small business, microcredit -- is an

00:36:31.369 --> 00:36:38.110
initiative that has found its time.

00:36:38.130 --> 00:36:44.880
I would go back, however, to a crucial need of education,

00:36:44.900 --> 00:36:48.119
which I think I heard my wife whispering on the right

00:36:48.139 --> 00:36:55.929
[laughter] And if I didn't hear her, she would have given me

00:36:55.949 --> 00:36:58.949
a hell of a time going back to New York this afternoon.

00:36:58.949 --> 00:36:59.949
[laughter]

00:36:58.949 --> 00:37:03.829
But education is clearly critical. Clearly critical.

00:37:03.849 --> 00:37:08.019
And that's boy's education, but more crucially, girl's

00:37:08.039 --> 00:37:09.039
education.

00:37:09.039 --> 00:37:14.619
And those two things, I think, are fundamental to what could

00:37:14.639 --> 00:37:16.589
happen in Africa.

00:37:16.609 --> 00:37:20.360
So you have a lot of changes occurring in Africa.

00:37:20.380 --> 00:37:23.449
You have the China/India thing of which I spoke.

00:37:23.469 --> 00:37:26.969
You have a gradual move towards better, more-responsible

00:37:26.989 --> 00:37:28.650
government.

00:37:28.670 --> 00:37:34.150
And you have civil society moving in broader ways than it

00:37:34.170 --> 00:37:35.170
has previously.

00:37:35.170 --> 00:37:39.549
Part of the problem, however, is that there are just too

00:37:39.569 --> 00:37:43.679
many institutions in civil society that keep falling all

00:37:43.699 --> 00:37:45.389
over each other.

00:37:45.409 --> 00:37:49.170
And the overheads are just killing.

00:37:49.190 --> 00:37:52.829
I would estimate that for every hundred dollars that gets

00:37:52.849 --> 00:38:01.110
put aside for development, you'd be lucky if 30 to 35 cents

00:38:01.130 --> 00:38:05.480
gets to the project in every dollar.

00:38:05.500 --> 00:38:09.159
And the rest is taken up by overhead, stealing, and

00:38:09.179 --> 00:38:11.159
mismanagement.

00:38:11.179 --> 00:38:15.319
And that is a hugely important issue, which I've been trying

00:38:15.339 --> 00:38:19.179
to deal a little bit since I left the Bank, to try and see

00:38:19.199 --> 00:38:22.369
how you could get a greater rationalization of the aid

00:38:22.389 --> 00:38:23.799
business.

00:38:23.819 --> 00:38:27.750
And just have a free pickup -- a free pickup -- of 20

00:38:27.769 --> 00:38:31.299
percent of whatever it is that you're spending.

00:38:31.319 --> 00:38:35.619
But again, that's a problem for all of you to face.

00:38:35.639 --> 00:38:38.389
It's a nice, easy one I leave you. Yes, sir.

00:38:37.829 --> 00:38:40.329
Q The Chinese are investing heavily in the natural resources

00:38:39.829 --> 00:38:40.829
in Africa.

00:38:40.829 --> 00:38:43.829
Do you see that as a opportunity for stability in the region

00:38:43.829 --> 00:38:46.079
or will that just allow the corrupt governments more

00:38:46.079 --> 00:38:47.079
opportunity to maintain power?

00:38:47.079 --> 00:38:53.190
A Well, I think both is the answer.

00:38:53.210 --> 00:38:57.869
It is an opportunity for improvement, but in some of the

00:38:57.889 --> 00:39:01.789
countries, it's pretty evident that the benefits are going,

00:39:01.809 --> 00:39:04.969
sadly, to a small group.

00:39:04.989 --> 00:39:08.980
And that's just a fact.

00:39:09.000 --> 00:39:11.460
I wish it were otherwise.

00:39:11.480 --> 00:39:18.309
And it's not very clear that the Chinese are, at this stage,

00:39:18.329 --> 00:39:22.230
insisting on social benefits as a result of their

00:39:22.250 --> 00:39:24.699
involvement in the countries.

00:39:24.719 --> 00:39:28.230
They're more interested in getting the minerals out than in

00:39:28.250 --> 00:39:32.940
participating in high-level debates about What is the

00:39:32.960 --> 00:39:35.980
purpose of economic development.

00:39:36.000 --> 00:39:40.989
Up to now, that has been the position of the Chinese.

00:39:41.009 --> 00:39:45.679
I know a number of people in Chinese leadership who would

00:39:45.699 --> 00:39:51.069
wish it to be otherwise and would like to bring about some

00:39:51.089 --> 00:39:54.819
change, but the way the Chinese system works -- even though

00:39:54.839 --> 00:39:59.389
I've been there many, many times -- is still opaque to me.

00:39:59.409 --> 00:40:04.089
And I think it needs a decision at the top, beyond just

00:40:04.109 --> 00:40:05.539
making some speeches about it.

00:40:05.559 --> 00:40:08.239
It needs to be pushed down there.

00:40:08.259 --> 00:40:11.409
I see a big representation of Chinese in the room.

00:40:11.429 --> 00:40:16.480
Maybe they can help you after this gathering and tell you

00:40:16.500 --> 00:40:18.389
the realities.

00:40:18.409 --> 00:40:21.409
But the big reality in China is to get control of natural

00:40:21.380 --> 00:40:22.989
resources.

00:40:23.009 --> 00:40:27.960
And even with its aid programs in Africa, important as they

00:40:27.980 --> 00:40:35.509
are, they tend to be more attention-getters, in my judgment,

00:40:35.529 --> 00:40:38.639
than they are substantive.

00:40:38.659 --> 00:40:42.159
It's not wholly true, but everywhere -- the president's

00:40:42.179 --> 00:40:46.589
house, the town hall, the sports stadium -- is done by the

00:40:46.609 --> 00:40:48.000
Chinese.

00:40:48.019 --> 00:40:51.269
It's a very good thing to do, but what is really needed

00:40:50.670 --> 00:40:53.190
underneath that, is, to try and change the societal

00:40:53.210 --> 00:40:54.670
structure.

00:40:54.690 --> 00:40:57.940
And I don't think, up to now, the Chinese have attempted to

00:40:57.230 --> 00:40:58.230
do that.

00:40:58.230 --> 00:41:01.389
And from my little experience that I told you, in terms of

00:41:01.409 --> 00:41:07.099
this real estate development I was doing in Ghana.

00:41:07.119 --> 00:41:12.110
It doesn't help to have 25 Chinese doing the development and

00:41:12.130 --> 00:41:15.599
no Ghanaians if you're talking about bringing along the

00:41:15.619 --> 00:41:24.079
economy. Yes.

00:41:24.099 --> 00:41:27.349
Q Piggybacking on that, what can the U.S. and Europe do to

00:41:27.349 --> 00:41:33.980
change the relationship with Red China now?

00:41:34.000 --> 00:41:37.000
Can you describe the problem with what do you see as a

00:41:36.500 --> 00:41:37.500
potential solution

00:41:37.500 --> 00:41:38.500
[inaudible]

00:41:37.500 --> 00:41:38.750
A I would say nothing.

00:41:38.750 --> 00:41:42.000
The only thing that they could do would be to get in there

00:41:41.500 --> 00:41:42.500
and compete.

00:41:42.500 --> 00:41:45.500
But they're not going to influence China to change its ways.

00:41:45.250 --> 00:41:47.750
And they're not going to influence African governments to

00:41:47.250 --> 00:41:48.250
change their ways.

00:41:48.250 --> 00:41:49.750
Because, the interest is economic development.

00:41:49.750 --> 00:41:52.750
Maybe the dean would have a different view, but I don't

00:41:52.500 --> 00:41:53.750
think that's the case.

00:41:53.500 --> 00:41:57.539
I think reality is that, at the moment, the money is in the

00:41:57.559 --> 00:42:02.789
hands of the economic growth powers that are looking at

00:42:02.809 --> 00:42:05.730
natural resources, particularly in Africa.

00:42:05.750 --> 00:42:08.889
The interest is fundamentally, natural resources.

00:42:08.909 --> 00:42:11.420
And in the case of some Indian companies, also retailing,

00:42:11.440 --> 00:42:15.319
where there is a big history of manufacturing and selling in

00:42:15.339 --> 00:42:16.339
Africa.

00:42:16.339 --> 00:42:20.400
But it is a -- if we're going to compete -- first of all, we

00:42:20.420 --> 00:42:27.559
have to create a group of leaders who give a damn about

00:42:27.579 --> 00:42:29.259
Africa.

00:42:29.279 --> 00:42:32.500
And then, create a group of managers that are prepared to

00:42:32.519 --> 00:42:38.819
live and work in Africa and who care about Africa and who

00:42:38.839 --> 00:42:41.339
see it as an economic possibility -- which it clearly is --

00:42:41.339 --> 00:42:44.799
but it's a little less transparent than some other economic

00:42:44.819 --> 00:42:46.539
activities.

00:42:46.559 --> 00:42:50.940
And in the case of China and India, they're there already,

00:42:50.960 --> 00:42:52.710
and they're throwing people at it.

00:42:52.670 --> 00:42:55.809
The growth in the China resident population has just been

00:42:55.829 --> 00:43:02.569
enormous in the last ten years.

00:43:02.589 --> 00:43:03.589
Q

00:43:03.589 --> 00:43:04.589
[inaudible]

00:43:03.589 --> 00:43:04.839
A Sure. Yes, ma'am.

00:43:04.589 --> 00:43:07.839
Q You mention that there's going to be a tremendous shift in

00:43:07.589 --> 00:43:09.089
power and wealth in the future.

00:43:09.089 --> 00:43:14.039
And I was wondering if you had any thoughts on, Are there

00:43:14.059 --> 00:43:17.969
some tenets or guideposts from the past that you think will

00:43:17.989 --> 00:43:21.239
help us move forward in the future and kind of chart our way

00:43:21.239 --> 00:43:22.239
through these unknown waters?

00:43:22.239 --> 00:43:27.389
A Well, this is what I was saying before.

00:43:27.409 --> 00:43:33.400
In my generation, for a good 40 years of my business

00:43:33.420 --> 00:43:40.309
activity, there was no challenge to the 80/20.

00:43:40.329 --> 00:43:43.829
It was, in fact, a little higher at the end of World War II,

00:43:43.159 --> 00:43:50.089
but basically 80/20 was the profound understanding that

00:43:50.109 --> 00:43:54.109
people had.

00:43:54.129 --> 00:43:57.629
I can only put the problem in front of you which is that, If

00:43:57.029 --> 00:44:02.519
80/20 becomes 35/65, and you added another 3 billion people

00:44:02.539 --> 00:44:05.940
to the planet, that's a very different world.

00:44:05.960 --> 00:44:14.960
And frankly, what worries me is that people are not learning

00:44:14.980 --> 00:44:16.880
about the history and culture of Asia.

00:44:16.900 --> 00:44:21.480
They're not learning the languages of Asia.

00:44:21.500 --> 00:44:27.109
And at least, to the limited extent that I know it --

00:44:27.129 --> 00:44:35.139
certainly, by the statistics -- our younger graduates are

00:44:35.159 --> 00:44:41.549
not prepared to put themselves in Asia.

00:44:41.569 --> 00:44:45.369
And so, I just hope that our educational system will do

00:44:45.389 --> 00:44:50.529
something about training our kids -- younger or primary

00:44:50.549 --> 00:44:54.710
school -- in both language and in culture and in history.

00:44:54.730 --> 00:44:57.690
And then, have them go through, so that at universities,

00:44:57.710 --> 00:45:03.609
they're already interested in it.

00:45:03.629 --> 00:45:06.879
I have an image -- three years ago, I gave a speech, or two

00:45:06.679 --> 00:45:09.179
years ago, I gave a speech at BeiDa University, Beijing

00:45:09.179 --> 00:45:12.059
University, 700 kids in the audience.

00:45:12.079 --> 00:45:17.480
Spoke in English, no translation -- no translation.

00:45:17.500 --> 00:45:21.630
And the most fantastic questions that you could possibly

00:45:21.650 --> 00:45:23.139
have.

00:45:23.159 --> 00:45:26.159
There is no place in the West where you could have 700

00:45:26.009 --> 00:45:31.309
people in an audience speaking Mandarin or Cantonese and

00:45:31.329 --> 00:45:35.319
have a Chinese speaker make such a speech.

00:45:35.339 --> 00:45:38.559
I'm not suggesting that we all -- that the only yardstick is

00:45:38.579 --> 00:45:40.079
going out and learning the language.

00:45:39.900 --> 00:45:43.769
But I am suggesting that, in terms of culture and in terms

00:45:43.789 --> 00:45:49.799
of direction, we are miles behind where we should be if we'

00:45:49.819 --> 00:45:54.039
re going to be competitive.

00:45:54.059 --> 00:45:56.809
Maybe different on the West Coast, but certainly on the East

00:45:55.809 --> 00:45:56.809
Coast.

00:45:56.809 --> 00:45:57.809
[laughter]

00:45:56.809 --> 00:45:58.059
One there and one there.

00:45:58.059 --> 00:46:00.809
Q With the proposed change in the economics of the global

00:46:00.809 --> 00:46:03.809
10, 20, 30 years, how do you see that shifting the military

00:46:03.809 --> 00:46:06.309
balance, and what sort of consequences when it comes to

00:46:06.309 --> 00:46:10.210
that?

00:46:10.230 --> 00:46:20.159
A Well, I think you're ready to get -- as you are now -- in

00:46:20.179 --> 00:46:27.119
our country, a lot of pressure for less intervention -- both

00:46:27.139 --> 00:46:28.690
financially and in human terms.

00:46:28.710 --> 00:46:33.759
But I must say that, in the last year, I can't point to any

00:46:33.779 --> 00:46:36.210
great advances.

00:46:36.230 --> 00:46:39.589
So I don't know the answer to that question.

00:46:39.609 --> 00:46:43.869
I would just think intellectually that it has to be true

00:46:43.889 --> 00:46:48.159
that, if the economic weight is shifting, shifting with that

00:46:48.179 --> 00:46:49.369
will be responsibility.

00:46:49.389 --> 00:46:53.929
And part of that responsibility will be military and

00:46:53.949 --> 00:46:56.670
intervention and the issue of peace.

00:46:56.690 --> 00:47:00.389
And that's a whole lot of subject about the balance between

00:47:00.409 --> 00:47:05.589
the United States, Russia, and China, which would require

00:47:05.609 --> 00:47:07.759
another hour to get into.

00:47:07.779 --> 00:47:11.029
And, even then, I'm not sure I could give you an answer.

00:47:10.839 --> 00:47:13.909
But I can say that, historically, economic power has nearly

00:47:13.929 --> 00:47:17.079
always led to military might.

00:47:17.099 --> 00:47:24.179
And so, if there is a huge shift in economic, chances are,

00:47:24.199 --> 00:47:29.319
you'll see a significant shift in military power as well.

00:47:29.339 --> 00:47:31.089
There was one other question. Yes, sir.

00:47:30.949 --> 00:47:40.089
Q For those of us interested in effecting change or getting

00:47:40.109 --> 00:47:50.500
involved in economic development, what would be your advice

00:47:50.519 --> 00:47:53.269
with regards to how we balance the decision to get involved

00:47:53.269 --> 00:47:55.269
in government side, multilateral side, nonprofit side, or

00:47:54.769 --> 00:47:55.769
private sector?

00:47:55.769 --> 00:47:57.769
How should we think about those trade ops?

00:47:57.769 --> 00:48:01.019
A I think you can have a good career and a good contribution

00:48:01.019 --> 00:48:02.519
in any one of those four.

00:48:02.519 --> 00:48:06.049
I don't think that it's essential that, if you're interested

00:48:06.069 --> 00:48:10.859
in economic development, that you need to be at a business.

00:48:10.879 --> 00:48:14.019
I think that you can be in business and still have an

00:48:14.039 --> 00:48:15.289
influence.

00:48:15.309 --> 00:48:18.059
I think you can have an influence in the U.N.

00:48:17.609 --> 00:48:20.359
I think you can have an influence in the World Bank.

00:48:20.359 --> 00:48:22.609
I think can have an influence in other international

00:48:22.619 --> 00:48:24.500
agencies.

00:48:24.519 --> 00:48:29.069
I think the important thing -- if I were to give advice to

00:48:29.089 --> 00:48:35.230
my own kids, if they were of an age -- is that, I would try

00:48:35.250 --> 00:48:38.750
very hard to have them go take a look and spend a year and

00:48:38.500 --> 00:48:43.889
at least learn something about Asia.

00:48:43.909 --> 00:48:47.529
If my kids had come to me, and said they wanted to go study

00:48:47.549 --> 00:48:52.210
at Oxford or the Sorbonne, I would be vigorous in suggesting

00:48:52.230 --> 00:48:59.210
to them that they go to China or India -- vigorous.

00:48:59.230 --> 00:49:04.400
And so, I think that your generation -- although this is a

00:49:04.420 --> 00:49:08.009
slightly older group than an undergraduate group -- but

00:49:08.029 --> 00:49:12.779
there's still an opportunity to understand the world that

00:49:12.799 --> 00:49:14.299
you're going to live in.

00:49:14.049 --> 00:49:18.469
It's a very different world than I grew up in.

00:49:18.489 --> 00:49:21.489
And I don't think there have been an adequate series of

00:49:21.009 --> 00:49:25.349
changes made in our educational system or in the aspirations

00:49:25.369 --> 00:49:32.109
of young people to recognize that it's a very different

00:49:32.129 --> 00:49:37.819
world when you're talking about 65 percent of global GDP

00:49:37.839 --> 00:49:42.369
being outside the OECD countries.

00:49:42.389 --> 00:49:45.569
And it's a very different world when China and India

00:49:45.589 --> 00:49:48.719
together have 50 percent of the global GDP.

00:49:48.739 --> 00:49:53.489
And when two-thirds of the middle class are in those

00:49:53.509 --> 00:49:59.190
countries, and alone a billion in China; that's a different

00:49:59.210 --> 00:50:00.909
world.

00:50:00.929 --> 00:50:03.469
And I fear that our educational system -- although I know

00:50:03.489 --> 00:50:06.449
nothing about the Stanford system, so I'm not making any

00:50:06.469 --> 00:50:10.719
comment -- but I do know something about some other places.

00:50:10.739 --> 00:50:15.679
I fear that our educational system has not caught up to the

00:50:15.699 --> 00:50:17.750
realities.

00:50:17.769 --> 00:50:24.299
And what is fascinating to me is how the realities of the

00:50:24.319 --> 00:50:28.609
composition of students and graduate students in our

00:50:28.629 --> 00:50:33.119
universities is reflecting the change that I've described,

00:50:33.139 --> 00:50:37.299
which is that there are many more Chinese, Indians, Asians

00:50:37.319 --> 00:50:42.420
studying to the benefit of the entire community.

00:50:42.440 --> 00:50:47.239
The same is not true in reverse, and I think it's a damn

00:50:47.259 --> 00:50:48.869
shame.

00:50:48.889 --> 00:50:53.609
And hopefully, there will be a growing tide of people that

00:50:53.629 --> 00:50:56.969
can understand that learning about Chinese culture and

00:50:56.989 --> 00:51:02.279
Indian culture and Asian culture is not going backwards.

00:51:02.299 --> 00:51:06.639
It is discovering something which is enormously rich,

00:51:06.659 --> 00:51:11.000
profound, and important, and has to be part of the balance

00:51:11.019 --> 00:51:12.029
of people's lives.

00:51:12.049 --> 00:51:13.779
Thanks very much.

00:51:13.799 --> 00:51:13.999
&gt;&gt;

00:51:12.799 --> 00:51:13.799
[Applause]

